# the argument of descartes-real-distinction.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Descartes, Discourse IV. "Could suppose" is read as what I could conceive or suppose, not as what could actually be,
# so the possibility claims are plain atoms rather than modal operators.
# The step from what can be supposed to what my essence is goes unstated in the text and is left as it stands.
atom	can_suppose_no_body	I could suppose that I had no body
atom	can_suppose_no_world	I could suppose that there was no world nor any place in which I might be
atom	can_suppose_not_exist	I could suppose that I was not
atom	think	I thought (to doubt of the truth of other things)
atom	exist	I was (existed)
atom	reason_believe_exist	I would have had reason to believe that I existed
atom	essence_thinking	I am a substance whose whole essence or nature consists only in thinking
atom	needs_no_place	I, that I may exist, have need of no place
atom	independent_material	I am not dependent on any material thing
atom	mind_distinct_body	the mind by which I am what I am is wholly distinct from the body
atom	mind_better_known	the mind is more easily known than the body
atom	survives_without_body	although the body were not, the mind would still continue to be all that it is
P1	S1	can_suppose_no_body ∧ can_suppose_no_world
P2	S1	¬can_suppose_not_exist
P3	S1	think → exist
P4	S1	think
# Counterfactual: had I ceased to think, I would have had no reason to believe that I existed, even if all other objects were real.
P5	S1	¬think → ¬reason_believe_exist
C1	S1	essence_thinking ∧ needs_no_place ∧ independent_material	from P1 P2 P3 P4 P5
C	S1	mind_distinct_body ∧ mind_better_known ∧ survives_without_body	from C1
